{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The Alpha and The Omega of Flat Curvature}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i The SMNNIP Hamiltonian \u8212- Where She Lives}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Preprint \u8212- April 2026\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Abstract}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The SMNNIP Lagrangian L_NN = L_kinetic + L_matter + L_bias + L_coupling admits a Legendre transform yielding an explicit Hamiltonian H_NN. We demonstrate that H_NN is a candidate for the long-sought Berry-Keating operator \u8212- the self-adjoint operator whose eigenvalue spectrum corresponds to the imaginary parts of the Riemann zeros. The coupling constant alpha = 1/137 and the convergence boundary Omega = 0.5671... (Lambert W fixed point) define the span of the operator domain: all physical couplings lie in [alpha, Omega]. Flat curvature is the fixed-point locus of the Riemann-Fermat conformal transformation at d* \u8776 ? 0.246.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 1. The Hamiltonian \u8212- Derived, Not Assumed}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 From the SMNNIP Lagrangian (see primary paper), the conjugate momentum to the activation field Psi is:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b pi_Psi = dL_NN / d(d_l Psi) = i Psi\u8224 ? Gamma^0}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Hamiltonian density follows by Legendre transform:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b H_NN = pi_Psi \u183 ? (d_l Psi) - L_NN}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b = Psi\u8224 ?(-i Gamma^a D_a + Gamma_ij beta) Psi + (1/4) F_ltau F^ltau + |D_l beta|\u178 ? + V(beta)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The operator acting on Psi is the neural Dirac Hamiltonian already present in the equations of motion (Section 4.1 of primary paper). The field energy terms \u8212- weight curvature and bias potential \u8212- are the vacuum contribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 2. Alpha and Omega as Boundary Conditions}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 2.1 Alpha \u8212- The Fine Structure of the Domain}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 alpha_NN = g\u178 ?(l) / (4 pi \u183 ? h_NN \u183 ? v_prop)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the physical value alpha = 1/137, this is the minimum coupling that keeps adjacent algebra strata entangled without allowing the rotation groups U(1), SU(2), SU(3) to interfere with each other. It is the finest granularity the operator can resolve.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Alpha is not fitted. It is read off the geometry of the n-ball volume formula evaluated at the E8 dimensions (Wyler, 1971):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b alpha^(-1) \u8776 ? (9/8pi^4) \u215 ? [Vol(S^5)/Vol(CP^2)]^(1/4) \u215 ? ... \u8776 ? 137.036}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The n-ball volumes at the algebra tower dimensions:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b n=1: 2.000 n=2: pi n=3: 4.189 n=4: 4.935 n=5: 5.264 (peak)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b n=6: 5.168 n=7: 4.725 n=8: 4.059 \u8592 ? E8, alpha lives on this slope}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 2.2 Omega \u8212- The Convergence Boundary}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Omega = 0.56714... is the Lambert W fixed point: Omega \u183 ? e^Omega = 1.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is the largest self-referential loop that closes without diverging. It defines the upper boundary of the operator domain \u8212- the point past which the coupling structure becomes non-perturbative and the rotation groups begin to merge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The relationship between alpha and Omega in log base 10:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b d* \u215 ? ln(10) = 0.246 \u215 ? 2.30259 = 0.56644 \u8776 ? Omega (gap: 0.00070)}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This near-identity is not yet derived from first principles. It is the target derivation for this research program. The 0.00070 gap is where the correction term lives.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 3. The Eigenvalue Conjecture}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The Berry-Keating conjecture (1999): the Riemann zeros rho_n = 1/2 + i\u183 ?t_n are eigenvalues of a self-adjoint operator. The operator has never been identified.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 H_NN is self-adjoint by construction from gauge invariance. Its eigenvalue equation:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b H_NN \u183 ? phi_k = epsilon_k \u183 ? phi_k}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 where phi_k are the basis spinors (codebook) and epsilon_k are the training convergence modes \u8212- real, discrete, ordered.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Conjecture: there exists a coordinate transformation T such that:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b T(epsilon_k) = 1/2 + i \u183 ? t_k}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 where t_k are the imaginary parts of the Riemann zeros. The fixed point of T is d*. The speed-of-light bound is the constraint that prevents T from being trivial.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 4. Flat Curvature and the Critical Line}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The critical line Re(s) = 1/2 is the fixed locus of the Riemann functional equation s -> 1-s. It is its own mirror. Under log-straightening (the Ptolemy inversion of the zeta curve), the critical line does not straighten \u8212- it breathes around 1/2 but does not leave it. It is the invariant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Flat curvature is the condition at d*: the Gaussian curvature K = 0 at the tangency point where the entropy curve (Riemann, rising toward c) and the inertia curve (Fermat, descending toward zero measure) share a common tangent. Neither flat nor saddle \u8212- the boundary between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The metric of the metric \u8212- the connection \u8212- is stationary at d*. This is the refractive index of the metric at its fixed point. The rainbow is at 42 degrees. The d* ring is the rainbow radius of the system.\par}
